1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:09,417
This is every security level in an American federal prison, from a camp with no fence to a cell where you spend 23 hours a day alone.

2
00:00:09,633 --> 00:00:16,889
About 150,000 people are in federal custody, and every one of them was sorted into one of these levels.

3
00:00:17,133 --> 00:00:21,953
And the most secure prison in the country is not even rated high security.

4
00:00:22,867 --> 00:00:23,486
Step one.

5
00:00:23,526 --> 00:00:24,219
The score.

6
00:00:24,433 --> 00:00:32,497
A judge can recommend a prison, but your level is calculated by the Bureau of Prisons, with points, on a single form.

7
00:00:32,733 --> 00:00:34,585
How serious the crime was.

8
00:00:34,625 --> 00:00:36,187
Your criminal history.

9
00:00:36,227 --> 00:00:37,133
Any violence.

10
00:00:37,173 --> 00:00:38,588
Any escape attempts.

11
00:00:38,628 --> 00:00:40,116
Each one adds points.

12
00:00:40,367 --> 00:00:43,629
For men, zero to eleven points means minimum.

13
00:00:43,669 --> 00:00:45,464
Twelve to fifteen is low.

14
00:00:45,504 --> 00:00:47,959
Sixteen to twenty-three is medium.

15
00:00:47,999 --> 00:00:49,941
Twenty-four and up is high.

16
00:00:50,167 --> 00:00:50,789
Quick test.

17
00:00:50,829 --> 00:00:52,414
You scored fourteen points.

18
00:00:52,454 --> 00:00:53,618
Where are you going?

19
00:00:57,167 --> 00:00:57,970
Low security.

20
00:00:58,010 --> 00:01:01,277
And just three points separate a camp from a fence.

21
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:06,698
Only one thing on the form can take points away: showing up to prison on your own.

22
00:01:06,933 --> 00:01:08,696
And the score is not final.

23
00:01:08,736 --> 00:01:14,637
Certain red flags, called public safety factors, can override it and push you up a level.

24
00:01:15,533 --> 00:01:16,195
Step two.

25
00:01:16,235 --> 00:01:17,520
Minimum security.

26
00:01:17,733 --> 00:01:19,409
The federal prison camp.

27
00:01:19,449 --> 00:01:25,487
Dormitories instead of cells, very few officers, and limited fencing, or none at all.

28
00:01:25,733 --> 00:01:34,555
Camps were designed that way on purpose, so inmates could work on landscaping and maintenance without checking in and out of a main prison.

29
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,324
And sometimes, they just leave.

30
00:01:37,364 --> 00:01:44,022
In 2021, the Associated Press found 29 escapes from federal custody in 18 months.

31
00:01:44,062 --> 00:01:48,157
Every single one was from a minimum security camp.

32
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:51,369
Nearly half of them had still not been caught.

33
00:01:51,409 --> 00:01:55,883
At some camps, a single officer was watching everyone during a shift.

34
00:01:56,133 --> 00:01:58,888
The Bureau of Prisons calls them walk aways.

35
00:01:58,928 --> 00:02:00,984
The law still calls them escapes.

36
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:02,720
Step three.

37
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:03,737
Low security.

38
00:02:03,967 --> 00:02:05,935
This is where the fences start.

39
00:02:05,975 --> 00:02:09,240
A low security prison is surrounded by two of them.

40
00:02:09,467 --> 00:02:11,663
Inside, it still looks like a camp.

41
00:02:11,703 --> 00:02:17,094
Most people sleep in dormitories or cubicles, and there are more staff watching them.

42
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:18,778
Step four.

43
00:02:18,818 --> 00:02:20,086
Medium security.

44
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:25,873
The fences get stronger, and electronic detection systems watch the space between them.

45
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:30,943
And this is the first level where most people live in cells instead of dorms.

46
00:02:30,983 --> 00:02:33,226
A door that locks, from the outside.

47
00:02:34,133 --> 00:02:34,885
Step five.

48
00:02:34,925 --> 00:02:35,994
High security.

49
00:02:36,233 --> 00:02:39,259
These are the United States Penitentiaries.

50
00:02:39,299 --> 00:02:46,960
Walls or reinforced fences, cells for one or two people, and the most staff per inmate of any regular level.

51
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,649
For most people in federal prison, this is the ceiling.

52
00:02:50,689 --> 00:02:52,870
There is no regular level above it.

53
00:02:53,767 --> 00:02:54,438
Step six.

54
00:02:54,478 --> 00:02:55,624
Administrative.

55
00:02:55,867 --> 00:03:01,026
This is the strangest level, because it is not really a level of danger at all.

56
00:03:01,066 --> 00:03:02,672
It is a level of purpose.

57
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:12,378
Administrative prisons hold people for a specific mission, like people still waiting for trial, or inmates who need serious medical care.

58
00:03:12,418 --> 00:03:15,365
They can hold every security level at once.

59
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,397
And one administrative prison is very different from the rest.

60
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:21,287
Step seven.

61
00:03:21,327 --> 00:03:22,688
A D X Florence.

62
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:28,953
Its full name is the Administrative Maximum facility, in Florence, Colorado.

63
00:03:28,993 --> 00:03:34,565
It opened in 1994, and it is often called the Alcatraz of the Rockies.

64
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:37,458
Each cell is about seven by twelve feet.

65
00:03:37,498 --> 00:03:41,302
The bed, the desk, and the stool are all poured concrete.

66
00:03:41,533 --> 00:03:47,879
The only window is about four inches wide, angled so you can see the sky, but not where you are.

67
00:03:48,133 --> 00:03:51,689
Inmates spend about 23 hours a day alone in that cell.

68
00:03:51,933 --> 00:03:56,689
It has held some of the most notorious criminals in America.

69
00:03:56,729 --> 00:03:59,407
Drug lord Joaquin El Chapo Guzman.

70
00:03:59,447 --> 00:04:02,685
Boston Marathon bomber Dzhokhar Tsarnaev.

71
00:04:02,725 --> 00:04:05,482
Oklahoma City bomber Terry Nichols.

72
00:04:05,733 --> 00:04:08,453
Since it opened, no one has ever escaped.

73
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:10,297
But here is the twist.

74
00:04:10,337 --> 00:04:19,152
According to a 2016 report, about 90 percent of its more than 400 inmates were not sent there for their original crime.

75
00:04:19,192 --> 00:04:24,063
They were sent there because of how they behaved in other prisons.

76
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:27,433
So the system is not really about what you did.

77
00:04:27,473 --> 00:04:34,521
It starts with a number on a form, and at the very top, it is decided by what you do once you are inside.

78
00:04:34,767 --> 00:04:37,075
How many points do you think you would score?

79
00:04:37,115 --> 00:04:38,328
Tell me in the comments.
